WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.800
This sea urchin embryo cell is in anaphase: its two sets of chromosomes are moving

2
00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:10.140
apart. Now it must cut itself in two, right between them.

3
00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:13.500
How does its surface know where the middle is?

4
00:00:17.033 --> 00:00:19.233
The cut happens at the cell surface.

5
00:00:20.473 --> 00:00:23.953
There the surface pinches in, making a groove called the furrow.

6
00:00:24.913 --> 00:00:27.473
But the thing that marks the middle sits deep inside.

7
00:00:28.793 --> 00:00:33.853
It is the spindle, a bundle of microtubules with a star-shaped aster at each end.

8
00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:36.747
There are three simple answers.

9
00:00:38.107 --> 00:00:41.387
One: the spindle pulls the surface in directly, by force.

10
00:00:42.327 --> 00:00:45.567
Two: an actin ring gathers at the middle on its own.

11
00:00:46.787 --> 00:00:49.047
Three: the spindle only sends a signal.

12
00:00:50.207 --> 00:00:53.967
The signal marks the surface, and the ring is built under that mark.

13
00:00:55.000 --> 00:00:58.780
To tell them apart, researchers watched a switch protein called RhoA.

14
00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.780
RhoA is on when it holds GTP, and off when it holds GDP.

15
00:01:05.260 --> 00:01:08.580
They added a glowing probe that sticks only to switched-on RhoA.

16
00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:17.993
A band of active RhoA, shown here in amber, lights up just under the membrane.

17
00:01:19.373 --> 00:01:22.613
One to four minutes later, the furrow starts underneath it.

18
00:01:23.653 --> 00:01:25.813
The same order was seen in frog embryos.

19
00:01:27.173 --> 00:01:28.713
The mark comes before the cut.

20
00:01:29.717 --> 00:01:34.177
Next, they blocked actin assembly with a drug, so no ring could be built.

21
00:01:35.437 --> 00:01:38.537
In sea urchin embryos, the amber band still formed.

22
00:01:39.817 --> 00:01:41.597
So the signal does not need the ring.

23
00:01:43.017 --> 00:01:44.017
Answer two is out.

24
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.620
Now the decisive test.

25
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:50.040
This cell has already begun to furrow.

26
00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.840
A blunt needle pushes the spindle to one side.

27
00:01:55.060 --> 00:01:58.140
What moves first: the furrow, the amber band, or nothing?

28
00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:08.143
Over a few minutes, active RhoA gathers over the spindle's new middle.

29
00:02:09.443 --> 00:02:11.343
Only then does the furrow follow it there.

30
00:02:12.523 --> 00:02:14.483
The band leads, and the furrow follows.

31
00:02:15.823 --> 00:02:20.383
That order argues against the spindle simply dragging the surface, and it supports

32
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:22.403
answer three: signal first.

33
00:02:23.567 --> 00:02:25.967
Here is the rule for these embryo cells.

34
00:02:27.067 --> 00:02:31.147
The cut goes where the anaphase microtubules place a band of active RhoA.

35
00:02:32.007 --> 00:02:34.987
Move the microtubules, and the band moves first.

36
00:02:36.287 --> 00:02:37.287
The furrow follows.

37
00:02:38.367 --> 00:02:43.067
Under the band, active RhoA turns on formins and promotes myosin II.

38
00:02:44.347 --> 00:02:46.287
Formins build long actin filaments.

39
00:02:47.787 --> 00:02:51.207
Myosin II, a motor, pulls those filaments past each other.

40
00:02:52.507 --> 00:02:56.747
In its simplest picture, the result is a ring that tightens like a drawstring.

41
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.940
Now the two daughters are joined only by a thin bridge.

42
00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:07.040
Cutting that bridge is a separate job.

43
00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:13.680
One question stays open: how do microtubules deep inside switch on a protein at the

44
00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:19.200
membrane? But where the cut went was settled earlier, where the microtubules placed

45
00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:19.700
the band.
